`T3` numbers: base cover = 7 leaves, refined cover = 10 leaves, cold build meshes all 10, incremental build meshes only the 4 new leaves — the other 6 are reused from cache. `T6` confirms a slot used by an un-finished frame is held on the retire list and cannot be handed out; after `onWorkDone(0)` it is reclaimed and carries generation 2.

---

## 4. Why each guarantee holds

- **Crack-free (R1/R6):** the 2:1 balance pass guarantees adjacent leaves differ by at most one level, so every T-junction is a single midpoint per coarse edge; `removeTJunctions` inserts that vertex into the coarse triangle and reaches a fixed point (T1 re-runs the detector and finds nothing).
- **Order independence (R2):** `canonicalLeaves()` is a deterministic function of the sorted rule set, independent of insertion order; the mesh hash is computed from canonicalized, sorted triangles. T2 randomizes order 12× including disappear/replace; T4 reverses the declaration order. Hashes match the batch reference.
- **Minimal rebuild (R3):** `meshLeaf` depends only on its own brick, so cached triangles stay valid when neighbours change; `sync()` meshes only keys absent from the cache. Stitching is a cheap deterministic post-pass.
- **No stale races (R4):** a slot read by submitted work is placed on `retire` and only returned to the free list by `onWorkDone(fence)`; the draw binds a `generation` and the shader/CPU guard rejects mismatches.
- **Strict budget (R5):** `SlotPool.free` is the single source of truth, and retired-but-in-flight slots are *not* in it, so `inUse + pendingRetire ≤ capacity` always holds; `acquire` returns `null` (skip this brick this frame) rather than over-allocating.
